Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Optional: Completion Comparison", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a3bc438f-153d-44b7-8cdd-c4fb9c32911c"],

Cell["\<\
These are completion-only wall times, excluding diagram and channel \
preparation. The old call supplied prepared channel rows directly. The new \
calls retain the original channel targets through the preparation reports. \
PreparationRemainders -> None disables the first remainder attempt; Automatic \
enables it. For five-point +++ with AllPhysical references: old 0.70 s, None \
0.79 s, Automatic 0.67 s. All three return 3 expanded terms with complexity 27.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c3e039d4-88da-4997-b31e-46e4d076fc5e"],

Cell["\<\
For five-point ++- with AllSpurious references, the old prepared-channel call \
was UNRESOLVED after 4.03 s. With original targets retained, None completed \
in 3.83 s and Automatic in 6.59 s. Both successful results have 14 expanded \
terms and complexity 130.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"243555e5-f808-49cd-9891-f5b4731e02c9"],

Cell["\<\
For six-point ++++ with AllPhysical references, the old call completed in \
62.23 s with 36 expanded terms and complexity 516. None completed in 20.25 s \
and Automatic in 28.24 s; both give 12 expanded terms and complexity 108. \
The retained remainder attempts did not improve these latter two examples \
and added some overhead before the compact fallback. They remain bounded \
alternatives, not a guaranteed speedup. All three new Automatic results \
passed independent physical, high-energy and Feynman comparisons. These \
measurements are examples, not timing guarantees.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0cb96605-c0e3-4935-b38f-96b5b99b8626"]

}, Closed]]

}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{
  Cell["e,E,A.+,A.+,A.+,A.-", "Chapter"],

Cell["Construct and Check", "Section", ExpressionUUID->"4eb6abff-e598-59fb-a7d6-39b1d927c816"],

Cell["Evaluate this cell after Setup. The wrapper generates and prepares the diagrams and factorization channels, completes and prepares the amplitude, then runs all final checks. The printed test report precedes the returned expression. The saved manual work remains available below. This larger example retains the earlier two-hour factorization budget; that limit is not a finishing-time guarantee.", "Text", ExpressionUUID->"5def643f-d347-50e0-a9fd-68b70ff235b3"],

Cell["qed6PPPM = ConstructAmplitude[\n  {\"e\", \"E\", \"A.+\", \"A.+\", \"A.+\", \"A.-\"},\n  diagramModel, factorizationModel,\n  \"PolePreference\" -> \"AllPhysical\",\n  \"FeynmanModel\" -> qedFeynmanModel]", "Input", ExpressionUUID->"883d3035-fc8e-5e0e-ae1a-657308bc023a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Earlier Step-by-Step Work (Optional)", "Section", ExpressionUUID->"c32d3089-5705-5d5b-a0d4-c8e7324435b0"],
  Cell[CellGroupData[{
    Cell["1. Choose the Process and References", "Section"],
    Cell["particles = {\"e\", \"E\", \"A.+\", \"A.+\", \"A.+\", \"A.-\"};\nreferenceChoice = \"AllSpurious\";", "Input"]
  }, Open]],
  Cell[CellGroupData[{
    Cell["2. Generate the Diagrams", "Section"],
    Cell["diagrams = CreateDiagrams[particles, diagramModel];\nrawAmplitudes = diagramAmplitudes[diagrams];\nLength[rawAmplitudes]", "Input"],
    Cell[CellGroupData[{
      Cell["View the diagrams", "Subsection"],
      Cell["CreateArrowDiagrams[diagrams]", "Input"]
    }, Closed]]
  }, Open]],
  Cell[CellGroupData[{
    Cell["3. Prepare Each Diagram", "Section"],
    Cell["preparation = PrepareAmplitudes[rawAmplitudes, diagramModel,\n  \"PolePreference\" -> referenceChoice];", "Input"]
  }, Open]],
  Cell[CellGroupData[{
    Cell["4. Complete the Physical Residues", "Section"],
    Cell["channels = FactorizationAmplitudeChannels[particles, factorizationModel];\nLength[channels]", "Input"],
    Cell["There are 24 diagrams and 14 physical channels. Completion automatically identifies legs 3, 4 and 5 as identical boson states from the ordered process and model; leg 6 has the opposite helicity. This mixed-helicity completion takes substantially longer than the earlier examples; the two-hour limit below is a budget, not a guaranteed finishing time. The independent Feynman amplitude is used only in the final check, never in the completion.", "Text"],
    Cell["factorization = CompleteFactorization[preparation, channels, factorizationModel];", "Input"]
  }, Open]],
  Cell[CellGroupData[{
    Cell["5. Verify the Completed Amplitude", "Section"],
    Cell["feynmanDiagrams = CreateDiagrams[particles, qedFeynmanModel];\nfeynmanPairs = diagramAmplitudes[feynmanDiagrams];\npreparedFeynman = PrepareAmplitudes[feynmanPairs, qedFeynmanModel,\n  \"PolePreference\" -> \"AllSpurious\"];", "Input"],
    Cell["Clear[constructedAmplitude, constructionChecks];\nIf[FailureQ[factorization] || factorization === $Failed,\n  factorization,\n  constructedAmplitude = factorization;\n  constructionChecks = CheckAmplitudes[\n    constructedAmplitude, channels, factorizationModel,\n    \"FeynmanAmplitudes\" -> preparedFeynman,\n    \"FeynmanModel\" -> qedFeynmanModel,\n    \"Output\" -> \"Details\"];\n  constructionChecks[\"Table\"]]", "Input"],
    Cell[CellGroupData[{
      Cell["Inspect the Completed Amplitude", "Subsection"],
      Cell["constructedAmplitude", "Input"]
    }, Closed]]
  }, Open]]

}, Closed]]
}, Open]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["e,E,A.+,A.+,A.-,A.+", "Chapter", ExpressionUUID->"7b807755-c098-5ca7-a7bf-1d1c77baf2f0"],

Cell["qed6PPMP = ConstructAmplitude[\n  {\"e\", \"E\", \"A.+\", \"A.+\", \"A.-\", \"A.+\"},\n  diagramModel, factorizationModel,\n  \"FeynmanModel\" -> qedFeynmanModel]", "Input", ExpressionUUID->"98d71f36-8972-575f-9c69-f4d9a25fa8f9"]

}, Open]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["e,E,A.+,A.+,A.-,A.-", "Chapter"],

Cell["Construct and Check", "Section", ExpressionUUID->"d0ea60f9-9fe8-596d-952e-fb7f22b7885f"],

Cell["ConstructAmplitude now completes this case using additional channel continuations generated from the model's known lower-point amplitudes. Every original channel and all final checks, including the independent Feynman calculation, pass. The earlier step-by-step work remains below. These settings disable optional identity-route enumeration; ordinary spinor reduction and declared numerator mass-shell reductions still run.", "Text", ExpressionUUID->"26039431-c89a-5ead-9e82-153f2d487e70"],

Cell["qed6PPMM = ConstructAmplitude[\n  {\"e\", \"E\", \"A.+\", \"A.+\", \"A.-\", \"A.-\"},\n  diagramModel, factorizationModel,\n  \"FeynmanModel\" -> qedFeynmanModel]", "Input", ExpressionUUID->"0819812a-ac06-5b2c-a5ba-f2599326e196"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Earlier Step-by-Step Work (Optional)", "Section", ExpressionUUID->"6a956bf9-90be-57bf-b5fb-abe80cc95654"],
   Cell["For this balanced-helicity example, the bounded diagram-correction \
search did not close the factorization constraints. The successful \
construction below instead sews complete known lower-point QED amplitudes \
with an explicitly justified [5,3> shift. It is a worked QED recursion, not a \
new universal completion algorithm.", "Text"], Cell["Physical residues, independent Feynman comparisons and the E^-2 high-energy test pass at the sampled points. The checker now handles both simple bracket poles and all 28 compound factors on exact conserved sample curves. These are numerical finiteness checks, not a global analytic cancellation proof. Evaluate the checks below for a fresh report.", "Text"],
   Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["1. Diagrams and Preparation", "Section"],
      Cell["particles = {\"e\", \"E\", \"A.+\", \"A.+\", \"A.-\", \
\"A.-\"};\ndiagrams = CreateDiagrams[particles, diagramModel];\nrawAmplitudes \
= diagramAmplitudes[diagrams];\nLength[rawAmplitudes]", "Input"],
      Cell["Keep the original 24 diagram expressions for comparison. Preparation \
in the next cell is optional and can be skipped to reproduce the compact \
result quickly. The lower-amplitude construction in step 3 does not use the \
prepared diagram expression as a fitted answer.", "Text"],
      Cell["preparation = PrepareAmplitudes[rawAmplitudes, diagramModel,\n  \
\"PolePreference\" -> \"AllSpurious\"];", "Input"],
      Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["View the diagrams", "Subsection"],
         Cell["CreateArrowDiagrams[diagrams]", "Input"]}, Closed]]}, Open]],
   Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["2. Physical Factorization Channels", "Section"],
      Cell["The factorization model already contains the complete three-, \
four- and five-point amplitudes. All 14 physical channels remain targets, \
including the six that do not separate the shifted photons.", "Text"],
      Cell["channels = FactorizationAmplitudeChannels[particles, \
factorizationModel];\nLength[channels]", "Input"]}, Open]],
   Cell[CellGroupData[
     {Cell["3. Construct from Eight Lower-Amplitude Products", "Section"],
      Cell["The example helper chooses the reference spinors before shifting, \
shifts the entire sewn lower-amplitude expressions, evaluates each of the \
eight separated channels at its pole, and divides by that channel's original \
physical propagator. The normalized sewn state sum already includes the \
sewing minus sign, exactly once. No Feynman result or fitted correction \
coefficients enter this construction.", "Text"], Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{hepc\
atDirectory, \"tests\", \"QED-5-point-residues.wl\"}]];\nconstructedAmplitude \
= qedBalancedSixPointAmplitude[factorizationModel];", "Input"],
      Cell[CellGroupData[
        {Cell["Why the Eight Terms Reconstruct the Amplitude", "Subsection"],
         Cell["Use q = |5>[3|, p5(z) = p5 + z q and p3(z) = p3 - z q. \
Equivalently, |5](z) = |5] + z|3] and |3>(z) = |3> - z|5>. The other spinors \
do not shift. Thus q^2 = q.p3 = q.p5 = 0: both shifted photons stay massless \
and total momentum is conserved.", "Text"], Cell["Before shifting, choose \
angle reference |5> for each positive-helicity photon and square reference \
|3] for each negative-helicity photon. These are z independent. In \
particular, the shifted polarization vectors for photons 3+ and 5- are both \
proportional to q. Their reference denominators <53> and [53] are unchanged \
because <55> = [33] = 0. In the sewn expressions this fixes the reference in \
each three-by-five channel; the four-by-four products are already Xi free.",
          "Text"], Cell["Every minimal-QED diagram has one electron line with \
four photon insertions. Along the hard fermion segment joining the two \
shifted photons, a propagator has numerator Pslash + z qslash + m and \
denominator D0 + 2 z q.P. All other vertices and external polarizations are z \
independent. If the shifted insertions are adjacent, qslash (Pslash + z \
qslash + m) qslash = 2 q.P qslash, since qslash^2 = 0. Dividing by D0 + 2 z \
q.P gives O(1/z). If they are separated, the leading qslash in each endpoint \
propagator is killed by the adjacent shifted-photon qslash. Each endpoint \
then supplies O(1/z), so those diagrams are at least O(1/z^2). Therefore \
every one of the 24 minimal-QED diagrams vanishes at complex z infinity; no \
boundary contribution is needed for this shift.", "Text"],
         Cell["For a cut C, set eta_C = number of leg 5 in C minus number of \
leg 3 in C. Its propagator is D_C(z) = D_C + z eta_C <5|P_C|3], so z_C = \
-D_C/(eta_C <5|P_C|3]). Only cuts with eta_C nonzero give finite-z poles. \
There are eight: {1,3}, {1,5}, {2,3}, {2,5}, {1,3,4}, {1,3,6}, {1,4,5}, \
{1,5,6}.", "Text"], Cell["Cauchy's theorem applied to A(z)/z gives A(0) = \
sum_C R_C(z_C)/D_C. Here R_C is the complete normalized product of the two \
lower-point amplitudes summed over the internal spin. The minus from the \
finite-z residue is canceled by z_C times the propagator slope; it is not \
another sewing sign. The six nonseparated physical channels occur inside the \
lower-point amplitudes in these eight terms. Adding them again as separate \
terms would double count. The checker still tests all 14 channels \
independently.", "Text"], Cell["This hard-line argument uses minimal QED and \
the stated shift and references. It is not a proof for arbitrary interactions \
or models. The numerical tests remain independent checks of the \
implementation and conventions.", "Text"]}, Closed]],
      Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Inspect the Eight Roots and Sewn States",
          "Subsection"], Cell["recursionDetails = \
qedBalancedSixPointAmplitude[factorizationModel,\n  \"ReturnDetails\" -> \
True];\nKeyTake[#, {\"Cut\", \"ShiftRoot\", \"ReferenceMode\"}] & /@ \
recursionDetails[\"Channels\"]", "Input"],
         Cell["recursionDetails[\"Channels\"][[1]]", "Input"]}, Closed]],
      Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Saved Constructed Amplitude", "Subsection"],
         Cell["This is the same eight-term expression, retained explicitly rather than through a temporary file or compressed cache. Me and EE are obtained from the selected factorization model when the cell is evaluated, so moving the repository does not hardcode an old model scope. This is a separate worked recursion example, rather than a fallback for automatic diagram completion. Run the checks below to validate this expression with the current code.", "Text"], Cell["savedConstructedAmplitude = With[{\n  \
qedSavedMass = ModelParameter[factorizationModel, \"Me\"],\n  qedSavedCharge \
= ModelParameter[factorizationModel, \
\"EE\"]},\n(-4*qedSavedCharge^4*(qedSavedMass*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\"\
, \"Square\", 3], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", \
4]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Angle\", 1], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 5]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Angle\", 2], \n      \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6]] + \
qedSavedMass*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6], \n      \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Square\", \
1], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \
\"Square\", 2], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]] - \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Angle\", 1], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 5]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Square\", 2], \n      \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]]*\n     (-((PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, \
4, 5]], qedSavedMass]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", \n          \
 6], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3], \n          Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", \
4]])/(SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], Mom[1], \n          \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]] + SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 5], Mom[4], \n          Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]])) + \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6], Mom[1], \n       \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4]] + SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 6], Mom[4], \n       Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4]] + \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6], Mom[5], \n       \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4]]) - SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \
\"Angle\", 2], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", \
6]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Square\", 1], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 4]]*(SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3], Mom[1], \n  \
     Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]] + SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3], Mom[4], \n       Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]] + \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3], Mom[5], \n       \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]]))*(SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3], Mom[1], \n     Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6]] + \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3], Mom[4], \n     \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6]] + SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3], Mom[5], \n     Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", \
6]])*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4], Mom[1], \n    \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]])/(PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, 4]], \
qedSavedMass]*\n   PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, 4, 5]], \
qedSavedMass]*PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, 4, 5, 3]], \n    \
qedSavedMass]*(PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, 5]], qedSavedMass] - \n    \
(PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, 4, 5]], \
qedSavedMass]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], \n       \
Mom[1], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", \
3]])/(SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], \n       Mom[1], \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]] + SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 5], \n       Mom[4], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]]))*\n   \
(PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, 4, 5, 6]], qedSavedMass] - \n    \
(PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, 4, 5]], qedSavedMass]*\n      \
(SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], Mom[1], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3]] + \n       SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], \
Mom[4], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]] + \n       \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], Mom[6], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3]]))/\n     (SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], \
Mom[1], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]] + \n      \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], Mom[4], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3]]))) - \n (4*qedSavedCharge^4*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3], Mom[1], \n    Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", \
6]]*(SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4], Mom[1], \n     \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]] + SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 4], Mom[3], \n     Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]] + \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4], Mom[6], \n     \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", \
5]])*(qedSavedMass*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4], \n      \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Angle\", \
1], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \
\"Angle\", 2], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5]] + \
qedSavedMass*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], \n      \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Square\", \
1], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \
\"Square\", 2], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4]] - \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Angle\", 1], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 6]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Square\", 2], \n      \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4]]*(SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 5], Mom[1], \n       Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]] + \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], Mom[3], \n       \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]] + SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 5], Mom[6], \n       Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]]) - \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Angle\", 2], \n      Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Angle\", 5]]*SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Spin\", \"Square\", 1], \n      \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 3]]*((PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle[1, 3, 6]], \
qedSavedMass]*\n        SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], \
Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 6]]*\n        \
SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Square\", 4], Spinor[\"Helicity\", \
\"Square\", 3]])/\n       (-SpinorChain[Spinor[\"Helicity\", \"Angle\", 5], \
